Subject: wasix: succeed at fd_sync on a directory
Last-Update: 2026-09-30

fd_sync on a directory descriptor answered EISDIR. fsync(2) accepts a
directory, and a program that needs a rename or a new entry to be
durable syncs the directory holding it afterwards, and may treat the
failure as fatal. On a file, fd_sync only drains the writes the handle
holds and never fsyncs on the host; a directory holds no pending
writes, so the call now succeeds as it does on a file with nothing
pending.

diff --git a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/fd_sync.rs b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/fd_sync.rs
index f1c4ad7..866cba1 100644
--- a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/fd_sync.rs
+++ b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/fd_sync.rs
@@ -53,7 +53,12 @@ pub fn fd_sync(mut ctx: FunctionEnvMut<'_, WasiEnv>, fd: WasiFd) -> Result<Errno
                     return Ok(Errno::Inval);
                 }
             }
-            Kind::Root { .. } | Kind::Dir { .. } => return Ok(Errno::Isdir),
+            // fsync(2) accepts a directory, which a program syncs to make
+            // a rename or a new entry durable. On a file this call only
+            // drains the writes the handle holds and never fsyncs on the
+            // host; a directory holds no pending writes, so it succeeds as
+            // a file with nothing pending does.
+            Kind::Root { .. } | Kind::Dir { .. } => {}
             // Linux fsync(2) returns EINVAL for fds "bound to a special file
             // (e.g., a pipe, FIFO, or socket) which does not support
             // synchronization.", mirror that behaviour
